* text=auto

# Scripts are checked out with LF on every platform.
#
# The repository is `* text=auto` and the machines that use it run with
# core.autocrlf=true, so without these rules a fresh clone hands out CRLF
# copies of every script.  Git for Windows' bash happens to tolerate the stray
# carriage return (verified: shebangs, `set --` argument lists and `x="$(...)"`
# assignments all still behave), but that is a property of this one shell, not
# of the scripts: bash on Linux, WSL and macOS strips nothing and dies with
#     $'\r': command not found
# The files are small and ASCII, so pinning LF costs nothing.
*.sh  text eol=lf
*.py  text eol=lf
